  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of various sources of federal employment data, including OPM's FedScope database, Employment and Trends publication, Common Characteristics of Government report, and the President's Budget from the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). The report explains that while these sources offer valuable data on the size and characteristics of the federal workforce, they differ in methodology and frequency of data collection.\n\nFedScope is OPM's primary database for federal employment data, which includes information on executive departments, independent agencies, and the legislative and judicial branches. The data is available in various formats, including statistical tables and custom reports.\n\nEmployment and Trends is an annual publication that provides detailed information on the size and characteristics of the federal workforce, including trends, occupations, education levels, and age distribution. The report also includes caveats and considerations for calculating workforce totals.\n\nCommon Characteristics of Government is an annual report that outlines OPM's federal employee databases and includes frequently requested data.\n\nThe President's Budget from OMB includes detailed financial information on individual programs and appropriations accounts, as well as federal employee statistics created using the Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) counting method. The budget is published in four volumes, including Analytical Perspectives, Appendix, and Historical Tables, which contain various types of employment data.\n\nThe report emphasizes the importance of understanding the differences in methodology and data collection frequency when using federal workforce statistics from these sources.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of various sources of federal employment data, including OPM's FedScope database, Employment and Trends publication, Common Characteristics of Government report, and the President's Budget from the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). The report explains that while these sources offer valuable data on the size and characteristics of the federal workforce, they differ in methodology and frequency of data collection.\n\nFedScope is OPM's primary database for federal employment data, which includes information on executive departments, independent agencies, and the legislative and judicial branches. The data is available in various formats, including statistical tables and custom reports.\n\nEmployment and Trends is an annual publication that provides detailed information on the size and characteristics of the federal workforce, including trends, occupations, education levels, and age distribution. The report also includes caveats and considerations for calculating workforce totals.\n\nCommon Characteristics of Government is an annual report that outlines OPM's federal employee databases and includes frequently requested data.\n\nThe President's Budget from OMB includes detailed financial information on individual programs and appropriations accounts, as well as federal employee statistics created using the Full-Time Equivalent (FTE) counting method. The budget is published in four volumes, including Analytical Perspectives, Appendix, and Historical Tables, which contain various types of employment data.\n\nThe report emphasizes the importance of understanding the differences in methodology and data collection frequency when using federal workforce statistics from these sources.",
  "score": 20.540540081577806,
  "answers": [
    "This report describes online tools, reports, and data compilations created by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) that contain statistics about federal employees and the federal workforce. The report also describes key characteristics of each resource and briefly discusses selected methodological differences, with the intention of facilitating the selection of appropriate data for specific purposes. This report is not intended to be a definitive list of all information on the federal workforce. It describes significant and recurring products that contain specific data often requested by Members or congressional staff."
